                0.713,
                0.448
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.5: Circles intersecting each other at two points"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.465,
            0.848,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What does this tell us about the existence of a triangle? The points A and B along with either of the points of intersection of the circles will give us the required triangle. Thus, there exists a triangle having sidelengths 4 cm, 5 cm and 8 cm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.548,
            0.293,
            0.571
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.574,
            0.847,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Which of the following lengths can be the sidelengths of a triangle? Explain your answers. Note that for each set, the three lengths have the same unit of measure."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.634,
            0.63,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) 2, 2, 5 (b) 3, 4, 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.659,
            0.63,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(c) 2, 4, 8 (d) 5, 5, 8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.683,
            0.663,
            0.704
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(e) 10, 20, 25 (f) 10, 20, 35"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.707,
            0.353,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(g) 24, 26, 28"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.731,
            0.848,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We observe from the previous problems that "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "whenever there is a set of lengths satisfying the triangle inequality (each length < sum of the other two lengths), there is a triangle with those three lengths as sidelengths",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.815,
            0.846,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Will triangles always exist when a set of lengths satisfies the triangle inequality? How can we be sure?"
            }
          ]
        },
